{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RP-06 D6.1 Option-B impact assessment completed against current main `3b34d8b449`. New offline artifacts: `.joe-janitor/rp06-option-b-impact-probe.py` and `.joe-janitor/rp06-option-b-impact.md`; corrected the over-generalized code-review failure row in `rp06-chronology-failure-critique.md`. No tracked/production files changed.\n\nKey correction: code review does not uniformly convert a local finder failure to hollow INDETERMINATE. In a full offline gate trace, an advisory `UnretryableOutputError` at `docs` made calls `base -> docs`, then escaped `produce_code_review_verdict`; the successful base output was lost and `tests`, project discovery, verify, and coach did not run. A systemic `LLMUnavailableError` at the same point made the same two calls and returned hollow INDETERMINATE with `llm_ran=false`. Healthy trace made six calls: base, docs, tests, current project criterion, verify, coach. The project criterion's future membership remains subject to D6.2 and does not affect the stop/loss observation.\n\nPlan-review probe injected a realistic exhausted-output failure into advisory A1 and blocking E2. Both became empty findings, both wrote checkpoint `[]`, and both projected PASS after an otherwise clean downstream path. Current effective plan registry has 17 blocking and 33 advisory criteria before routing; code review has one blocking LLM built-in (security), advisory LLM built-ins, project-selected posture, and required/blocking-capable base coverage.\n\nDebt conclusion: A is not inherently structural debt because the gate-owned policy seam and legacy adapter are useful for shadow comparison and rollback. Shipping A as the final policy would be behavioral debt: it newly codifies (1) signed plan PASS with missing blocking coverage and (2) code-review loss/abort behavior that conflicts with the cross-gate drop-and-continue intent in jira-reb-687 / authorial-hated-blackbear. Recommended refinement: choose B as the end state while retaining A only as shadow/rollback policy during expand-contract.\n\nActual B boundary: only after RP-01's bounded recovery is exhausted and a unit is classified local. Systemic/provider outage, auth, policy/prompt/config errors, cancellation, DET fail_mode, scheduler/cache modes, and retry mechanics stay unchanged. Independent units continue; dependent units skip with provenance; no same-run retry of the failed unit. Advisory-only gap may PASS; blocking-capable gap is unsigned INDETERMINATE unless an actual blocking finding takes precedence as BLOCK. Plan call count normally stays the same because it already continues; code review may make later calls (measured example: up to four more, plus existing Round-B cap up to three). Positive explicit budgets bound this; omitted code-review budget remains uncapped."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787073385399479001,"uuid":"762fbdd6-e113-424c-a8a5-4510b2720419"}